%N A398469 Lexicographically earliest sequence such that, for any k, the indices at which k occurs form a geometric progression of length 3 or greater, which continues as long as the next index remains an integer and isn't already claimed by a previously established progression.
%C A398469 A progression is considered established once its first two terms have occurred.
%C A398469 Indices of 1s are given by A000079.
%C A398469 Every progression beginning at a prime index (A000040) or a squarefree index (A005117) has common ratio 2.
%H A398469 Neal Gersh Tolunsky, <a href="/A398469/b398469.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%e A398469 a(25) = 5 because this is the next index at which 5 occurs in i = 9,15,25 (common ratio 5/3). The progression ends there and this is the last 5 in the sequence because with a common ratio of 5/3, the next index would be 41 and 2/3rds, which is not an integer.
%e A398469 a(480) = 132 because the 132s at i = 270, 360, 480 have common ratio 4/3. The progression ends there because the next index in the progression i = 640 (480 * 4/3 = 640) is already claimed by value 3.
%Y A398469 Cf. A005117, A013929.
%K A398469 nonn,new
%O A398469 1,3
